#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403543 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "47989094-35d2-561f-add4-f1d427cd8a20" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Position Vector Given a Velocity Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "783ded5c-224b-59ae-b32b-ec19000aa60d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨{t}^{3}-{t}^{2}+2,\\,2{t}^{4}-42⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403545} {:answer/id #uuid "06d02cfb-4919-5c32-955c-bd2e1e9b658c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨{t}^{3}-2{t}^{2}+2,\\,{t}^{4}-24⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403546} {:answer/id #uuid "ad5eefc7-8bc8-5e3a-b849-484cdc6a8daa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨{t}^{3}-{t}^{2}+2,\\,2{t}^{4}-24⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403547} {:answer/id #uuid "f970c01f-ad2a-5aad-bf67-ace81ba81fd2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨{t}^{3}-{t}^{2},\\,2{t}^{4}-42⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403548} {:answer/id #uuid "dd440fad-abbb-55d5-b8ef-41b50f1b49d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨{t}^{3}-{t}^{2}+12,\\,2{t}^{4}-72⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403549}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dd440fad-abbb-55d5-b8ef-41b50f1b49d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨{t}^{3}-{t}^{2}+12,\\,2{t}^{4}-72⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403549} :answer-field/id #uuid "a072aa51-1711-5bcb-b3f7-621b3a6f46ec" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403544}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4e1070d0-6a3b-5c88-bdfe-23bfbc7b2d02" :question/math-academy-id "q-7217" :question/problem "The velocity $v$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\nv=⟨t(3t-2),\\,8{t}^{3}⟩.\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle is at the position $(30,90)$ when $t=3,$ calculate the position vector of $P$ at time $t.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the position vector, we need to integrate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨t(3t-2),\\,8{t}^{3}⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int t(3t-2)\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\int 8{t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{t}^{3}-{t}^{2}+{C}_{1},\\,2{t}^{4}+{C}_{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $r(3)=⟨30,90⟩.$ Substituting $t=3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(3) & =⟨(3)^{3}-(3)^{2}+{C}_{1},\\,2(3)^{4}+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨30,90⟩ & =⟨18+{C}_{1},162+{C}_{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have ${C}_{1}=12$ and ${C}_{2}=-72.$ Therefore, the position vector is\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨{t}^{3}-{t}^{2}+12,\\,2{t}^{4}-72⟩.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079202 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "279c5e4e-db19-584b-becb-10864fcba188" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Position Vector Given an Acceleration Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3fb179c5-0c95-5eb7-b275-9ca460f5d11c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨{t}^{3}-2{t}^{2}+4t,\\,{t}^{2}-2t+6⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403551} {:answer/id #uuid "cbbdc519-f4c7-5a08-a7dd-3f88bedcf9c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨2{t}^{3}-2{t}^{2},\\,{t}^{2}-t⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403552} {:answer/id #uuid "3a4412af-fcc5-5a70-8cc1-058a234b657a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨{t}^{3}-{t}^{2},\\,2{t}^{2}-2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403553} {:answer/id #uuid "4f5fe75f-182c-5dc8-bb6d-6b2a54417938" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨{t}^{3}-t,\\,2{t}^{2}-2t+6⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403554} {:answer/id #uuid "4892e60d-0f8b-54a6-a9fb-aedd25ed041e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨{t}^{3}+4t,\\,{t}^{2}-t⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403555}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3a4412af-fcc5-5a70-8cc1-058a234b657a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨{t}^{3}-{t}^{2},\\,2{t}^{2}-2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403553} :answer-field/id #uuid "66b0a37a-393b-5f66-b851-8ee2229ca9f6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403550}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "83a0e0a1-0c0d-5f48-8e86-38cbeffbfb71" :question/math-academy-id "q-7221" :question/problem "The acceleration $a$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\na(t)=⟨6t-2,4⟩.\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle is at the position $(0,0)$ and has velocity $⟨1,\\,4⟩$ when $t=1,$ calculate the position vector of $P$ at time $t.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the position vector, we need to integrate the velocity vector. To find the velocity vector, we first need to integrate the acceleration vector. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨6t-2,4⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int (6t-2)\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\int 4\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3{t}^{2}-2t+{C}_{1},\\,4t+{C}_{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $v(1)=⟨1,4⟩.$ Substituting $t=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(1) & =⟨3(1)^{2}-2(1)+{C}_{1},\\,4(1)+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨1,4⟩ & =⟨1+{C}_{1},4+{C}_{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have ${C}_{1}=0$ and ${C}_{2}=0.$ Therefore, the velocity vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =⟨3{t}^{2}-2t,\\,4t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can compute the position vector by integrating the velocity vector.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨3{t}^{2}-2t,\\,4t⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int (3{t}^{2}-2t)\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\int 4t\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{t}^{3}-{t}^{2}+{C}_{3},\\,2{t}^{2}+{C}_{4}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $r(1)=⟨0,0⟩.$ Substituting $t=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(1) & =⟨(1)^{3}-(1)^{2}+{C}_{3},\\,2(1)^{2}+{C}_{4}⟩ \\\\ ⟨0,0⟩ & =⟨{C}_{3},2+{C}_{4}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have ${C}_{3}=0$ and ${C}_{4}=-2.$ Therefore, the position of $P$ at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨{t}^{3}-{t}^{2},\\,2{t}^{2}-2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403556 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "79daf9dd-814d-562b-b3bc-b066bd2dfb90" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Shift Applied to an Exponential Growth Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "93a712bc-5c5d-5646-9703-82e77d69b46e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-3({3}^{x})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403558} {:answer/id #uuid "c63beba4-7686-58ae-845d-111254d3e6ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={3}^{x}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403559} {:answer/id #uuid "5996d30b-a352-5547-9b35-f09224ff8616" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={3}^{x}-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403560} {:answer/id #uuid "e2d5db3c-106b-5a20-8512-215f226e23f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={3}^{x}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403561} {:answer/id #uuid "3d9e24e9-f8f1-50d2-a309-392b65ff2168" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-2({3}^{x})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403562}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e2d5db3c-106b-5a20-8512-215f226e23f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={3}^{x}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403561} :answer-field/id #uuid "7f6e4f21-8993-51e5-a1c9-737356240b0e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403557}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bbca085f-0b60-5f9a-bdc1-1da6491170fe" :question/math-academy-id "q-7230" :question/problem "![](images/87/8764149ca0000522277d48bf48f4ef14ba06f7e4793ba1a58ef639ff7af3a3b9.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the curve $y={3}^{x}+c.$ Determine the value of $c$ and find the equation of the curve." :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote of $y={3}^{x}$ is $y=0.$ The vertical translation $y={3}^{x}+c$ shifts the asymptote to $y=c.$\n\nIn the graph above, the horizontal asymptote is $y=-2.$ Therefore, $c=-2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403563 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "79daf9dd-814d-562b-b3bc-b066bd2dfb90" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Shift Applied to an Exponential Growth Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "760d4f2f-67c3-578b-90ce-e44b5cc0e475" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={2}^{x}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403565} {:answer/id #uuid "a42ff124-ce1b-58ec-a4c4-728cb4b536df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-3({2}^{x})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403566} {:answer/id #uuid "9bb95f56-7efa-56a4-ab5b-5f4220d4b140" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={2}^{x}-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403567} {:answer/id #uuid "840799e0-019f-5e56-960e-e4dce9f6a43e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={3}^{x}-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403568} {:answer/id #uuid "97c4002b-68c4-5b92-bb46-3a3c52c7bbac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3({2}^{x})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403569}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9bb95f56-7efa-56a4-ab5b-5f4220d4b140" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={2}^{x}-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403567} :answer-field/id #uuid "117f36b7-d2dd-58cf-81f2-744857f29ebf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403564}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bb2d08ab-7e1f-579d-8314-e037ebc00133" :question/math-academy-id "q-7232" :question/problem "![](images/2a/2ad72b3df34f32d6a76f79f6801887bd6fe1d762fefd74071517a754b1890ac2.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the curve $y={2}^{x}+c.$ Determine the value of $c$ and find the equation of the curve." :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote of $y={2}^{x}$ is $y=0.$ The vertical translation $y={2}^{x}+c$ shifts the asymptote to $y=c.$\n\nIn the graph above, the horizontal asymptote is $y=-3.$ Therefore, $c=-3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403570 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7948909f-8dcb-5cc0-a00f-f505a41e4004" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying a Region as Type I, Type II, Both, or Neither"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cebe51cf-7c64-56b5-90cf-d131aa350434" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403572} {:answer/id #uuid "bb98efc4-1808-5a18-907a-2ed362b46813" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403573} {:answer/id #uuid "715194f5-7e2b-5553-92e1-418bb1db22f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403574} {:answer/id #uuid "52989cfd-9a0b-5436-a602-9bec29db010b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403575} {:answer/id #uuid "23c11e6d-f7bd-5794-99cd-309344d7fab8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403576}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "52989cfd-9a0b-5436-a602-9bec29db010b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403575} :answer-field/id #uuid "1886eec5-845d-5de6-8b01-c5885246a233" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403571}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d94b1132-4667-52b0-83fe-bd8235741b13" :question/math-academy-id "q-72345" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the finite region bounded between the line $y=x$ and the curve $y={x}^{3},$ in the first quadrant?\n\nI. It is a type I region\nII. It is a type II region\nIII. It's neither a type I nor a type II region" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that\n\n$$\ny={x}^{3}\\,⟹\\,x=\\sqrt[3]{y}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's sketch our region.\n\n![](images/1d/1d1c1cbe30e6f612f824adc76d712a98194ffccc5edefc11cccdaf07204513ba.png)\n\nWe have the following:\n\n- Any vertical line through the region intersects it on a single interval. Furthermore, we can represent our region as\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,{x}^{3}\\le y\\le x}.\n$$\n\nSo, this is a type I region.\n\n- Any horizontal line through the region intersects it on a single interval. Furthermore, we can represent our region as\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,y\\le x\\le \\sqrt[3]{y}}.\n$$\n\nSo, this is a type II region.\n\nTherefore, only statements I and II are true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403577 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "84245283-3cf9-5889-8c42-896a0c8af291" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Points on a Curve at which the Tangent Vector is Perpendicular to a Given Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b3b898a6-e9f5-51f8-8993-b6f0322af5e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(3,2)$ and $(-1,3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403579} {:answer/id #uuid "3acac861-9675-5531-8e0f-29301bd84beb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,1)$ and $(-1,-1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403580} {:answer/id #uuid "879099ba-26c3-5eca-b156-1546984f09e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,2)$ and $(-1,-2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403581} {:answer/id #uuid "56e7a278-62d5-5448-adb4-7672d93764ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-1,2)$ and $(1,-2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403582} {:answer/id #uuid "c66b876c-2af7-5756-8122-0570c4918dbd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,3)$ and $(3,-2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403583}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "879099ba-26c3-5eca-b156-1546984f09e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,2)$ and $(-1,-2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403581} :answer-field/id #uuid "22c59322-d13f-5287-a0fa-c2f86091a95e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403578}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "49dc4b38-854b-53b5-ba44-1bf47c27ef1c" :question/math-academy-id "q-72412" :question/problem "At which points on the curve ${y}^{2}-4xy=-5{x}^{2}+1$ is the tangent vector to the curve perpendicular to the vector $i?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-4xy & =-5{x}^{2}+1 \\\\ {y}^{2}-4xy+5{x}^{2} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)={y}^{2}-4xy+5{x}^{2}$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=1.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\nt(x,y)={f}_{y}(x,y)\\,i-{f}_{x}(x,y)\\,j\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y) & ={f}_{y}(x,y)\\,i-{f}_{x}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}-4xy+5{x}^{2})i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2}-4xy+5{x}^{2})j \\\\  & =2(y-2x)\\,i-(10x-4y)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince our tangent vector needs to be perpendicular to $i$, we must have $t\\cdot i=0.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y)\\cdot i & =0 \\\\ (2(y-2x)\\,i-(10x-4y)\\,j)\\cdot i & =0 \\\\ 2(y-2x) & =0 \\\\ y & =2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $y=2x$ into the equation of our curve gives:  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-4xy+5{x}^{2} & =1 \\\\ (2x)^{2}-4x(2x)+5{x}^{2} & =1 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}-8{x}^{2}+5{x}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =1 \\\\ x & =\\pm 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally:\n\n- If $x=1$, we get $y=2$ and the corresponding point is $(1,2).$\n\n- If $x=-1$, we get $y=-2$ and the corresponding point is $(-1,-2).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403584 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2d390841-351e-54f5-ad19-90b6ebae5799" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Matrices in Row Echelon Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "037eeb73-f010-5239-b320-397bc7facb77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403586} {:answer/id #uuid "48b44963-1902-5c0d-9522-c534c3678776" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403587} {:answer/id #uuid "0eeb9a23-f447-585f-9585-f82e287918e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403588} {:answer/id #uuid "c1989825-0208-5721-8305-7b2993765c83" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403589} {:answer/id #uuid "610ceecf-c4a9-5885-8266-6104d4f37cf1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403590}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "037eeb73-f010-5239-b320-397bc7facb77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403586} :answer-field/id #uuid "d8c3e5f4-9bc1-505b-a784-0a6ba8cc7e8a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403585}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "307a31ed-3320-5bc6-9033-1067b69032de" :question/math-academy-id "q-72491" :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are **not** in row echelon form?\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 5 & 1 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 5 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 5 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -7 & 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that a matrix is in row echelon form if it satisfies the following conditions:\n\n- All the zero rows (rows with only zeros), if any, are placed at the *bottom* of the matrix.\n- Each leading entry of a row is in a column *to the right* of the leading entry of the row above it.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each matrix in turn.\n\n- The matrix $A$ is not in row echelon form since the leading entry ${a}_{43}\\ne 0$ (condition II is violated). \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- The matrix $B$ is in row echelon form (both conditions are satisfied).\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 5 & 1 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- The matrix $C$ is not in row echelon form since the leading entry ${c}_{32}\\ne 0$ (condition II is violated). \n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 5 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 5 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -7 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, only $A$ and $C$ are **not** in row echelon form."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403591 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2d390841-351e-54f5-ad19-90b6ebae5799" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Matrices in Row Echelon Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d2449f17-fb24-5f21-b93a-91da7cb73b6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403593} {:answer/id #uuid "f82dd2a1-af9f-5176-9521-a602fb86b66c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403594} {:answer/id #uuid "a73d0fd1-33f8-5fef-91ee-18ea7bc00233" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403595} {:answer/id #uuid "1e393d24-d14c-55d4-9c1c-d1fb4487bb44" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403596} {:answer/id #uuid "0a24998d-88d6-567d-b060-eecb883f1a8b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403597}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1e393d24-d14c-55d4-9c1c-d1fb4487bb44" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403596} :answer-field/id #uuid "80541a17-4cbb-5d55-a794-14a52b470ccb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403592}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ba6bcd83-87df-5c0b-b121-63a0ff1205ca" :question/math-academy-id "q-72492" :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are **not** in row echelon form?\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that a matrix is in row echelon form if it satisfies the following conditions:\n\n- All the zero rows (rows with only zeros), if any, are placed at the *bottom* of the matrix.\n- Each leading entry of a row is in a column *to the right* of the leading entry of the row above it.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each matrix in turn.\n\n- The matrix $A$ is in row echelon form (both conditions are satisfied).\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- The matrix $B$ is in row echelon form (both conditions are satisfied).\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- The matrix $C$ is not in row echelon form since the leading entry ${c}_{22}\\ne 0$ (condition II is violated). \n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, only $C$ is **not** in row echelon form."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403598 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "66ade855-4b16-514b-b44a-1d6b9d61ef28" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Shift Applied to an Exponential Decay Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "128902a2-01ba-574b-8cb8-9e24d91588d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403600} {:answer/id #uuid "d421b62c-91bc-51bd-9a1e-dcb674541fa2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403601} {:answer/id #uuid "89b95e28-4bdc-577f-9fef-39eb015deed9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403602} {:answer/id #uuid "1f9a6792-46dd-54a0-852a-c3ce900255d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403603} {:answer/id #uuid "cdf02042-68e8-57bc-821d-c4e709f6e6d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403604}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1f9a6792-46dd-54a0-852a-c3ce900255d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403603} :answer-field/id #uuid "86226cb6-da89-5070-bdaf-be3482566831" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403599}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c80543b3-4997-5175-82f2-660b5af23e52" :question/math-academy-id "q-7253" :question/problem "![](images/50/50be1f4a894e86b5f06fba18655caca163c3d4c7a3423d2ecf159aa49975eb8c.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the curve $y={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}+c.$ Determine the value of $c$ and find the equation of the curve." :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote of $y={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}$ is $y=0.$\n\nThe vertical translation $y={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}+c$ shifts the asymptote to $y=c.$\n\nIn the graph above, the horizontal asymptote is $y=1.$ Therefore, $c=1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403605 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6381faa4-5dfd-58fa-ace7-411fd2a97e98" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the x-Intercepts of an Ellipse By Completing the Square"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ef8edbea-4da2-562c-be95-1803e0e50af8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(5\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{3}{2}},0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403607} {:answer/id #uuid "e45d242e-ec58-51d9-b98e-0c301509d596" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(10\\pm \\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2},0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403608} {:answer/id #uuid "9141967a-e95f-5916-97f3-21bd825b34c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There is no $x$-intercept" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403609} {:answer/id #uuid "3903afea-29b9-5179-b7c5-dc45513572f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(5\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403610} {:answer/id #uuid "88c9f761-6944-512d-b426-301b6d2b5ff0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(10\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403611}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ef8edbea-4da2-562c-be95-1803e0e50af8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(5\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{3}{2}},0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403607} :answer-field/id #uuid "629087fb-db7f-51a8-827b-0862fb389bb9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403606}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3538d2b4-fd15-5d04-9456-067812f7d246" :question/math-academy-id "q-72913" :question/problem "Calculate the points where the ellipse $2{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}-20x+6y+47=0$ intercepts the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "To calculate the $x$-intercepts, we need to substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $x.$ We do this and solve the resulting equation by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}-20x+6y+47 & =0 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+3(0)^{2}-20x+6(0)+47 & =0 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-20x+47 & =0 \\\\ 2[{x}^{2}-10x]+47 & =0 \\\\ 2[(x-5)^{2}-25]+47 & =0 \\\\ 2(x-5)^{2}-50+47 & =0 \\\\ 2(x-5)^{2} & =3 \\\\ (x-5)^{2} & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ x-5 & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{3}{2}} \\\\ x & =5\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{3}{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $x$-axis at $(5-\\sqrt{\\frac{3}{2}},0)$ and $(5+\\sqrt{\\frac{3}{2}},0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403612 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d21d6066-f7c8-5a47-b150-d0ae25d2d60b" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Center and Axis Lengths Given an Inhomogeneous Polynomial Equation of an Ellipse"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7c381e28-47d1-516c-ba70-7941c1b4c573" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4$ and $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403614} {:answer/id #uuid "c7ab0ed2-8c80-5ee7-ad45-3c43ece3b795" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$8$ and $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403615} {:answer/id #uuid "e0ac736d-0463-57d0-9db1-2a7177ddc558" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$8$ and $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403616} {:answer/id #uuid "b2703eaa-98a6-5759-b55c-68a1aa801db2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4$ and $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403617} {:answer/id #uuid "cfd845e5-ca89-50e3-8d11-42ad65bcd599" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2$ and $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403618}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b2703eaa-98a6-5759-b55c-68a1aa801db2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4$ and $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403617} :answer-field/id #uuid "66032e6a-59fa-5bca-a1f1-2d6e69a901e8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403613}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a0bc6070-452c-59d7-b850-234ba4c8bcd8" :question/math-academy-id "q-72934" :question/problem "Determine  the lengths of the major and minor axes of the ellipse whose equation is ${x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}-4x+16y=-16.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we must group the $x$ terms and the $y$ terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}-4x+16y & =-16 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-4x)+(4{y}^{2}+16y) & =-16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must complete the square for both the $x$ and $y$ terms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}^{2}-4x)+(4{y}^{2}+16y) & =-16 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-4x)+4({y}^{2}+4y) & =-16 \\\\ [(x-2)^{2}-4]+4[(y+2)^{2}-4] & =-16 \\\\ (x-2)^{2}-4+4(y+2)^{2}-16 & =-16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rearrange the equation into the desired form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2}-4+4(y+2)^{2}-16 & =-16 \\\\ (x-2)^{2}+4(y+2)^{2} & =-16+16+4 \\\\ (x-2)^{2}+4(y+2)^{2} & =4 \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{1} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{{2}^{2}}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{{1}^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $2>1,$ the length of the major axis is $2\\cdot 2=4,$ and the length of the minor axis is $2\\cdot 1=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403619 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "64e7d2fb-003c-58c0-ab80-159e50748cc7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Intercepts of Spheres and Ellipsoids"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "adcb33db-de68-50f5-9cbe-6b7d8f84aaae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,0,0)$ and $(2,0,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403621} {:answer/id #uuid "ee548906-3638-5d51-96d2-b358b4a1f8b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-2,0,0)$ and $(4,0,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403622} {:answer/id #uuid "81a50ee6-a29f-5cf2-ba44-251758623d8b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-4,0,0)$ and $(4,0,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403623} {:answer/id #uuid "ef09c53b-97f7-594e-a8f7-fee68af6a063" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(4,0,0)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403624} {:answer/id #uuid "b4640bb5-2749-5d8c-bb08-ee4349a6f316" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-3\\sqrt{2},0,0)$ and $(3\\sqrt{2},0,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403625}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ee548906-3638-5d51-96d2-b358b4a1f8b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-2,0,0)$ and $(4,0,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403622} :answer-field/id #uuid "d82b2d36-5f6d-5949-984e-5d1d9ec7556d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403620}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fc3f69db-faba-5b23-aeec-3cea350fe1f0" :question/math-academy-id "q-73190" :question/problem "Given the ellipsoid $\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{18}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{8}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{16}=1$, find its intercepts with the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "To find the $x$-intercepts, we plug $y=0$ and $z=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{18}+\\frac{(0+2)^{2}}{8}+\\frac{{0}^{2}}{16} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{18}+\\frac{1}{2}+0 & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{18} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ (x-1)^{2} & =9 \\\\ x-1 & =\\pm 3 \\\\ x & =1\\pm 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we get the solutions $x=4$ and $x=-2.$\n\nTherefore, the intercepts with the $x$-axis are $(-2,0,0)$ and $(4,0,0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403626 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f8f3989e-bde0-5b3a-8d45-08f34d330c3f" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Intervals of Increasing or Decreasing Displacement"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dc080a13-8e0c-5832-9bea-08398f14a3f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[0,\\frac{1}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403628} {:answer/id #uuid "c0cca4dd-1e7a-5b07-a0de-042fce6f3d42" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{2},\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403629} {:answer/id #uuid "37d286fa-57c9-5bfb-a268-edcd61ddcadd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[0,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403630} {:answer/id #uuid "36bdbd94-1ad2-595f-a5b8-8b35a41f1e19" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[0,\\frac{1}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403631} {:answer/id #uuid "811209a9-afb1-5f47-8576-6515ea37eb32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{4},\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403632}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "36bdbd94-1ad2-595f-a5b8-8b35a41f1e19" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[0,\\frac{1}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403631} :answer-field/id #uuid "04f008a8-35a2-5209-a21c-d7193e72a599" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403627}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "08503449-abde-5746-b6d7-46180193efed" :question/math-academy-id "q-73210" :question/problem "The displacement $s,$ in meters, relative to a fixed origin $O$ of a particle moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis is given by $s(t)=t{e}^{-2t}$, where $t\\ge 0$ is the time, measured in seconds. Find the time intervals in which the particle is moving in the direction of increasing $x.$" :question/worked-solution "If the particle moves in the direction of increasing $x,$ then its velocity is positive. \n\nFirst, we calculate the velocity function by differentiating the position function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t{e}^{-2t}) \\\\  & ={e}^{-2t}(1-2t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate time intervals in which the particle is moving in the direction of increasing $x$, we solve $v(t)>0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & >0 \\\\ {e}^{-2t}(1-2t) & >0 \\\\ 1-2t & >0 \\\\ 2t & <1 \\\\ t & <\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the time interval in which the particle is moving to the direction of increasing $x$ is $t\\in [0,\\frac{1}{2}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097356 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cd47a43f-1e99-5909-aa48-da8690025197" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a 3x3 System of Equations Using Standard Gaussian Elimination with Row Exchanges"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "affc0ad4-801a-5399-8a47-b7526facc7d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "M∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403634} {:answer/id #uuid "ccb21bbb-79ee-5df3-9904-5df560da18c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "M∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{1}{3} & \\frac{7}{3} \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403635} {:answer/id #uuid "c5805566-431b-59b1-b5d4-027282fdf8a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "M∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{1}{2} & -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403636} {:answer/id #uuid "ae4c1b86-80d7-59f5-af00-319d8bf12b68" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "M∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{2}{3} & -\\frac{7}{3} \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403637} {:answer/id #uuid "76bc4cc9-6acc-5be4-bff8-66f385a06fe3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "M∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -\\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403638}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ae4c1b86-80d7-59f5-af00-319d8bf12b68" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "M∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{2}{3} & -\\frac{7}{3} \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403637} :answer-field/id #uuid "f15e152b-e602-5625-9a15-cb468ccf6dd8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403633}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e85abf79-3b58-57ed-b3d6-9f31cc3b2817" :question/math-academy-id "q-73397" :question/problem "Consider the augmented matrix $M$ given by\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 5 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 4 & 9\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to row echelon form." :question/worked-solution "Reducing $M$ to row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 5 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 4 & 9\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 4 & 9\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -2 & 7\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we get ${a}_{22}=0.$ Therefore, we swap the $2$nd and $3$rd rows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -2 & 7\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & ↔{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -2 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we multiply the $2$nd row by $-\\frac{1}{3}$ and the $3$rd row by $\\frac{1}{2}$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -2 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=-\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{2}{3} & -\\frac{7}{3} \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{2}{3} & -\\frac{7}{3} \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403639 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6b5ea4a7-acc4-5825-b6c5-b6c87e6a1674" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Critical and Stationary Points of a Function Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17bd2520-0c4f-54a1-9032-4feba8ac4cd7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=6$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403641} {:answer/id #uuid "bf955400-4c85-5799-a0bb-34bb8034740d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=2$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403642} {:answer/id #uuid "fab62fe8-12aa-52f0-b98d-2406eb0e0b56" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403643} {:answer/id #uuid "b01d168e-efd5-5ced-839e-03be5807a102" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=2$ and $x=6$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403644} {:answer/id #uuid "9102896f-bd20-58cb-a6f3-3d67a1deeab9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "All points in the closed interval $[2,6]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403645}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9102896f-bd20-58cb-a6f3-3d67a1deeab9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "All points in the closed interval $[2,6]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403645} :answer-field/id #uuid "f7a88cb6-2519-5806-b90d-f5beabf35ae2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403640}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6fc62285-d6ad-563b-a202-c3fe4f3d9e71" :question/math-academy-id "q-73409" :question/problem "![](images/7c/7c5bc75101a3bf4b4074bf60c1ab68962f2ed0de559f889365c3807be2fa833c.png)\n\nConsider the function $y=f(x)$ whose graph is shown above. What are the $x$-coordinates of all the critical points?" :question/worked-solution "The critical points of $f(x)$ are the values of $x$ where ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(x)$ doesn't exist. Graphically, those are the values where the tangent to the graph is horizontal or where the tangent does not exist, respectively.\n\nIn the given graph, we can see that\n\n- at any point between $x=2$ and $x=6$ the tangent to the graph is horizontal, so we have ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ while\n\n- at $x=2$ and at $x=6$ there are sharp corners, hence ${f}^{′}(2)$ and ${f}^{′}(6)$ don't exist.\n\n![](images/6e/6e4299d762e26ff3de85ae0fd766d28368de937098dfcf232ce9a234fee826e1.png)\n\n  \n\nTherefore, all numbers in the closed interval $[2,6]$ are critical points for $f(x).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403646 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6b5ea4a7-acc4-5825-b6c5-b6c87e6a1674" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Critical and Stationary Points of a Function Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cb4d5bbd-8b8e-5b87-b552-49eb950fa340" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=3$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403648} {:answer/id #uuid "25a9ad5d-e3da-5b19-ae3c-1a4da3e2def8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=3$, $x=4$, and $x=6$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403649} {:answer/id #uuid "4424aa1e-fbe6-5341-b6bf-db50547251d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "All points in the open interval $(3,4)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403650} {:answer/id #uuid "d8f5049a-410b-5e0f-aa9d-73bdc98cfb29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=4$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403651} {:answer/id #uuid "ae9b2849-1a87-535e-a5be-0fdab3635438" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=3$ and $x=4$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403652}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4424aa1e-fbe6-5341-b6bf-db50547251d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "All points in the open interval $(3,4)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403650} :answer-field/id #uuid "8aaf3855-a51d-5ca4-8c0a-99b3bc89ed52" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403647}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a831b1fe-cda9-5ab9-bf01-7acc19edee37" :question/math-academy-id "q-73410" :question/problem "![](images/ea/eab84ed04b86678fb4a8d23c010e9a22beead062595b49818ac95e996c3173e6.png)\n\nConsider the function $y=f(x)$ whose graph is shown above. What are the $x$-coordinates of all the stationary points?" :question/worked-solution "The stationary points of $f(x)$ are the values of $x$ where ${f}^{′}(x)=0.$ Graphically, those are the values where the tangent to the graph is horizontal.\n\nIn the given graph, we can see that at any point between $x=3$ and $x=4$ the tangent to the graph is horizontal, so we have ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at those points.\n\n![](images/ab/ab200bebf80bbd1abe6e0c43ec124fe359da25f38d4346d25d8741decc88aa26.png)\n\n  \n\nTherefore, the $x$-coordinates of the stationary points of $f(x)$, are all values in the open interval $(3,4).$ \n\n**Note:** At $x=3$ and $x=4$, there are sharp corners, so ${f}^{′}(x)$ doesn't exist at those points. This gives us two more critical points. But they are not stationary points."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403653 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7948909f-8dcb-5cc0-a00f-f505a41e4004" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying a Region as Type I, Type II, Both, or Neither"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "58331f85-b5fc-59a5-a2ca-f0a6fd38ca0b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403655} {:answer/id #uuid "76022809-5efc-5b86-8507-f2ad2e7674e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403656} {:answer/id #uuid "b0765542-63c4-5076-85eb-d92c5b5ac592" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403657} {:answer/id #uuid "20d4565f-595d-51c6-857d-208b49c021b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403658} {:answer/id #uuid "57eafbb0-6829-567e-b846-7bde9a6cae27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403659}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "20d4565f-595d-51c6-857d-208b49c021b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403658} :answer-field/id #uuid "c8f0581f-5142-59ed-84df-f95cc7adf20e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403654}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "964e90c7-3ddc-59ef-9c19-6cc6da31e561" :question/math-academy-id "q-73552" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the finite region bounded between the ellipses $(x-1)^{2}+4{y}^{2}=1$ and ${x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}=4?$\n\nI. It is a type I region\nII. It is a type II region\nIII. It's neither a type I nor a type II region" :question/worked-solution "First, let's sketch our region.\n\n![](images/21/215dd0c920e700a75896fc54751ec20d197487a2c992cd8910810c66129c14dc.png)\n\nWe have the following:\n\n- The vertical line $x=1$ intersects the region in two separate intervals, as shown below. So, this can't be a type I region.\n\n![](images/7e/7e1a0c5dd2b4aadf7efe78d903234b8d00cf4fa92eb7a3b41209a856f2d14b01.png)\n\n- The horizontal line $y=0.25$ intersects the region in two separate intervals, as shown below. So, this can't be a type II region.\n\n![](images/a9/a90d7306e3f88a31609e368f9362248d6a12b7922b6a9e8c9f980ab31e7fb140.png)\n\nTherefore, only statement III is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403660 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cd47a43f-1e99-5909-aa48-da8690025197" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a 3x3 System of Equations Using Standard Gaussian Elimination with Row Exchanges"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1bbb2c9b-2424-5248-830c-2c1f0a65a08a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "M∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & 12 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403662} {:answer/id #uuid "090537bb-6f40-5836-9945-a970355e758b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "M∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403663} {:answer/id #uuid "fab64420-cace-555b-aa98-7c62e22daa9b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "M∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & -4 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -9 & 12 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403664} {:answer/id #uuid "bb59ea2b-37e2-5ba0-85a8-5281bf3bc593" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "M∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403665} {:answer/id #uuid "24b18c66-2b96-5dd6-a905-03f61b72ec8a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "M∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403666}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bb59ea2b-37e2-5ba0-85a8-5281bf3bc593" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "M∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403665} :answer-field/id #uuid "45e63128-e41c-54ad-afda-6808e4f902e4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403661}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "09ed8746-0452-52a7-a619-929b2d587f13" :question/math-academy-id "q-73580" :question/problem "Consider the augmented matrix $M$ given by\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 20 & -10 & -5 \\\\ -2 & -8 & 6 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -9 & 12\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to row echelon form." :question/worked-solution "Reducing $M$ to row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}5 & 20 & -10 & -5 \\\\ -2 & -8 & 6 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -9 & 12\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{5}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & -2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & -8 & 6 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -9 & 12\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -9 & 12\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we get ${a}_{22}=0.$ Therefore, we swap the $2$nd and $3$rd rows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -9 & 12\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & ↔{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -9 & 12 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we multiply the $2$nd row by $\\frac{1}{3}$ and the $3$rd row by $\\frac{1}{2}$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -9 & 12 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403667 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "488c9762-554b-5ea6-b414-ce6cbde9bbd1" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Pivot Columns of a Matrix in Row Echelon Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "50e1027b-7acb-51f2-acfa-7f635f2c3e05" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3$rd only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403669} {:answer/id #uuid "c3a71a14-cbd2-522b-997b-fd450972744e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1$st and $3$rd only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403670} {:answer/id #uuid "5c7527ea-3828-5e2d-bf88-c0fe30aaa76e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2$nd and $3$rd only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403671} {:answer/id #uuid "fdea791f-9fdb-55f9-aceb-2d7e1234420c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1$st, $2$nd, and $3$rd" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403672} {:answer/id #uuid "186dbf5e-ae6b-51d6-bcec-ca5ddbec9d8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2$nd only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403673}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5c7527ea-3828-5e2d-bf88-c0fe30aaa76e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2$nd and $3$rd only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403671} :answer-field/id #uuid "89c5cdb3-5a82-57f9-a194-5bfb67b87de1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403668}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0708b0a5-1bfe-57b2-8400-87bacda18799" :question/math-academy-id "q-73606" :question/problem "The row echelon form matrix $B$ is given below. What are the pivot columns of $B?$\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the pivots of a matrix in row echelon form are the first non-zero entries in each row of the matrix.\n\nWe can see that $B$ has $2$ pivots in the $2$nd and $3$rd columns:\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403674 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5f0d2e9-1cf6-5fe4-85cc-f401382f39b2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Non-Pivot Columns of a Matrix in Row Echelon Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f5d1b21-d0b9-5d69-8355-827e89736e7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2$nd and $4$th only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403676} {:answer/id #uuid "ae1f6f68-236b-5baf-b98e-ebb13a837d0c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4$th only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403677} {:answer/id #uuid "2d838c13-5fd7-5baf-875a-cbdb0a8f06c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2$nd, $3$rd, and $4$th only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403678} {:answer/id #uuid "b50399a2-2bb0-5c2b-b2df-c9a80a6714cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2$nd, $4$th, and $5$th only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403679} {:answer/id #uuid "e320a2ba-1f1d-5701-ac7a-db08a7b45731" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2$nd only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403680}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0f5d1b21-d0b9-5d69-8355-827e89736e7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2$nd and $4$th only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403676} :answer-field/id #uuid "bfd951bf-1101-57d3-9237-a2969bb45e55" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403675}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "701f0d82-dd3a-58b3-b64b-6042ce1e752a" :question/math-academy-id "q-73612" :question/problem "The row echelon form matrix $B$ is given below. What are its **non**-pivot columns?\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 2 & 1 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -4 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the pivots of a matrix in row echelon form are the first non-zero entries in each row of the matrix.\n\nWe can see that $B$ has $3$ pivots in the $1$st, $3$rd and $5$th columns:\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 2 & 1 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -4 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the only non-pivot columns are the $2$nd and $4$th columns."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403681 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3cbf0ad0-a7c5-5c0f-a06c-9210fdc244ef" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Range of a Parametric Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b9f73d83-c051-5dff-95b7-3c8c97c9edbb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge -1,\\,y\\ge 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403683} {:answer/id #uuid "ad14150b-a7fa-5e1f-8e26-8118219d8796" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge 0,\\,y\\le -4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403684} {:answer/id #uuid "aa0317e7-34d8-5e8a-a56c-876d04157a55" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge 1,\\,y\\le -4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403685} {:answer/id #uuid "0117c445-1ef3-5ae6-aae5-46c6b2367762" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\le 4,\\,y\\ge 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403686} {:answer/id #uuid "a9339c84-b774-56f5-95e7-f320ba73550e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\le 2,\\,y\\ge -4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403687}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a9339c84-b774-56f5-95e7-f320ba73550e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\le 2,\\,y\\ge -4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403687} :answer-field/id #uuid "966a4be9-e62e-532a-b5f4-b29c14204104" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403682}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5fc2dccb-24dd-5003-b2b4-73d21d258e08" :question/math-academy-id "q-73617" :question/problem "Given that a curve is defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=1-t,\\,\\,\\,y={t}^{2}-4,\\,t\\ge -1,\n$$\n\ndetermine the ranges of $x$ and $y.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider each variable in turn.\n\n- To find the range of $x,$ note that $1-t\\le 2$ for all $t\\ge -1.$ Consequently, $x=1-t\\le 2.$\n\n- To find the range of $y,$ note that ${t}^{2}\\ge 0$ for all $t.$ Consequently, $y={t}^{2}-4\\ge -4.$\n\nTherefore, the ranges are $x\\le 2$ and $y\\ge -4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403688 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b1a557d6-52f3-5df3-8088-5b875296e72e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Pivot Columns of a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "62c7b061-ae07-587c-bab0-15049cf99b56" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1$st and $4$th only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403690} {:answer/id #uuid "bc30072d-7cf1-5da5-97b9-253a92f7877b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2$nd and $3$rd only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403691} {:answer/id #uuid "508efe11-c663-52c3-84b8-ac714fe99937" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1$st, $3$rd, and $4$th only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403692} {:answer/id #uuid "8054e257-e2ab-5aa6-b464-152bde3bafb3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1$st, $2$nd, $3$rd, and $4$th only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403693} {:answer/id #uuid "56527d12-3319-58cd-8ff8-f8efdf4d8acf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1$st, $2$nd, and $4$th only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403694}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8054e257-e2ab-5aa6-b464-152bde3bafb3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1$st, $2$nd, $3$rd, and $4$th only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403693} :answer-field/id #uuid "6197e392-74ff-5689-be7c-741e44610b04" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403689}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8bbb774c-4bbb-58f8-84cb-fd20b0029b02" :question/math-academy-id "q-73638" :question/problem "What are the pivot columns of the matrix $P,$ given by\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 & 1 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 5 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to convert $P$ to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 & 1 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 5 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & ↔{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 & 1 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 5 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & ↔{R}_{4} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 & 1 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 5 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLooking at the row echelon form, we see that $P$ has $4$ pivots. In particular, the pivot columns are the $1$st, $2$nd, $3$rd, and $4$th columns."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403695 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "97cd9132-458b-5234-a3d2-22b633acb74c" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing an Augmented Matrix for a Singular 2x2 System to REF and Solving the Resulting System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22cd6aa9-a925-5038-975c-07e44d80e350" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1-2y \\\\ y\\end{aligned}],\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403697} {:answer/id #uuid "0e0364cb-677b-524e-aa38-691178112aba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2+y \\\\ y\\end{aligned}],\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403698} {:answer/id #uuid "6274e549-fa07-5abb-bd6c-53630d47a7b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1+2y \\\\ y\\end{aligned}],\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403699} {:answer/id #uuid "11509ba5-2bfa-5670-b24e-690396084002" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2-y \\\\ y\\end{aligned}],\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403700} {:answer/id #uuid "da9d2dd8-4de7-59f6-8ae5-f0b3a20d355b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1+2y \\\\ y\\end{aligned}],\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403701}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "22cd6aa9-a925-5038-975c-07e44d80e350" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1-2y \\\\ y\\end{aligned}],\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403697} :answer-field/id #uuid "0ccee7c5-bc00-583c-adcd-616a8af7768b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403696}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "28d32302-16b0-5cdc-a69e-754fd94318a8" :question/math-academy-id "q-73740" :question/problem "Consider the augmented matrix $M$ given by \n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -5 & -10 & 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce $M$ to row echelon form, and then find the general solution of the linear system represented by the matrix." :question/worked-solution "Reducing $M$ to echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -5 & -10 & 5\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+5{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x+2y=-1 \\\\ 0x+0y=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince any pair of numbers $x,y$ is a solution of the final equation $0x+0y=0,$ we can remove this equation from the system. So, our system now consists of a single equation\n\n$$\nx+2y=-1.\n$$\n\nThe first variable $(x)$ is the basic variable, and the second variable $(y)$ is the free variable. Solving for the basic variable $(x)$ in terms of the free variable $(y),$ we get $x=-1-2y,$ and the general solution is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=-1-2y \\\\ y=y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $y$ is an arbitrary number. \n\nIn vector form, the general solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1-2y \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403702 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ddc6c4b1-b4c4-52f8-9f25-feda3130e358" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Intercepts of a Parabola Given Its Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ade6b736-21fc-5927-801a-6f95d4712df7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,13)$ and $(0,-7)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403704} {:answer/id #uuid "2cda79e2-68f3-5c41-896a-04084cb1b5f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,-13)$ and $(0,7)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403705} {:answer/id #uuid "f8befb49-e008-5058-9add-edd4e46f5252" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,-13)$ and $(0,-7)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403706} {:answer/id #uuid "3a777482-4e1e-57a1-bc18-958db548f36c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(13,0)$ and $(0,-7)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403707} {:answer/id #uuid "2651a385-0c6f-559e-bc57-1a1a3107749a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,13)$ and $(0,7)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403708}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ade6b736-21fc-5927-801a-6f95d4712df7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,13)$ and $(0,-7)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403704} :answer-field/id #uuid "a708c3e3-24dc-5816-8cc2-c47d9443150d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403703}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "40490cf3-305d-5b56-bda2-cd96ef68cf95" :question/math-academy-id "q-73769" :question/problem "Find the points of intersection of the parabola ${y}^{2}-16x-6y-91=0$ with the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "When the parabola intersects the $y$-axis, we must have $x=0.$ So, we set $x=0$ and solve the resulting equation for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-16(0)-6y-91 & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2}-6y-91 & =0 \\\\ (y-13)(y+7) & =0 \\\\ y & =13,-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parabola intersects the $y$-axis at $(0,13)$ and $(0,-7).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403709 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8c990d69-54da-5541-948f-0d5eff96feb5" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the X-Intercepts of a Parabola Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "544b7ae8-40b2-5ec8-83d6-d19730e6b5a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$0$  and $\\sqrt{5}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403711} {:answer/id #uuid "2316f0bc-0936-5acf-bd1b-fe6117ca3155" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$0$ and $5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403712} {:answer/id #uuid "1bacfa54-8104-51a9-b2bd-c3c2a3c703ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$0$ and $3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403713} {:answer/id #uuid "019cfb67-fa2a-5255-b2d3-9c68213d29fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$0$ and $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403714} {:answer/id #uuid "8cdd38f7-11c0-5b67-b2ce-d02618e9c7a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$0$ and $2\\sqrt{5}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403715}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "019cfb67-fa2a-5255-b2d3-9c68213d29fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$0$ and $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403714} :answer-field/id #uuid "199aaf87-46d2-5438-b5f3-ee557ec29887" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403710}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "166cdda3-01a4-5828-9800-429450a46515" :question/math-academy-id "q-73832" :question/problem "![](images/e7/e79a3a7c2fce5a3e3ec7d838ebe1327dcc5452168f499fad05eea85a08e8f8b0.png)\n\nWhat are the $x$-intercepts of the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the equation of the parabola. From the diagram, we see that the parabola has a vertex at $(2,-1)$ and passes through the point $(-2,3).$\n\nThe general standard equation of an up- or down-opening parabola with vertex $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $(h,k)=(2,-1)$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2} & =4p(y+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find $p.$ Since the parabola passes through the point $(-2,3),$ we substitute $x=-2$ and $y=3$ into the equation and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-2-2)^{2} & =4p(3+1) \\\\ 16 & =4p(4) \\\\ p & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2} & =4(y+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen the parabola intersects the $x$-axis, we must have $y=0.$ So, we substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the parabola and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2} & =4(0+1) \\\\ (x-2)^{2} & =4 \\\\ x-2 & =\\pm \\sqrt{4} \\\\ x & =\\pm 2+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $x$-intercepts are $0$ and $4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403716 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9cc25edf-9d32-5b47-ae4f-e370d2d0d024" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points at Which Tangent Planes Have Particular Properties"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "90f4afcf-4af1-5320-a876-c7e43193b7cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-1,-1,1)$ and $(1,-1,-3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403718} {:answer/id #uuid "aaeb48d9-f69d-5b9e-9626-640bfefc395e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,-1,-3)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403719} {:answer/id #uuid "98696df4-b0fb-53ef-a177-bce372e2f606" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,1,0)$ and $(-1,1,2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403720} {:answer/id #uuid "1cffc4be-00ee-57b7-9ac9-95e61bc1ca5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,-1,2)$ and $(-1,-1,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403721} {:answer/id #uuid "479f131a-35b5-598d-93be-4ae6e4b42f61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-1,-1,1)$  only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403722}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "90f4afcf-4af1-5320-a876-c7e43193b7cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-1,-1,1)$ and $(1,-1,-3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403718} :answer-field/id #uuid "29a46a2f-b76d-5223-bfa0-caee05f36438" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403717}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "14749651-a6a1-55fa-b209-fec427b8a368" :question/math-academy-id "q-73860" :question/problem "At which points on the surface $z={x}^{3}-3x+{y}^{2}+2y$ is the tangent plane horizontal?" :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z=f(x,y)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ on the surface can be written as\n\n$$\nz-{z}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\n$$\n\nwhere $⟨\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),-1⟩$ represents a normal vector to the tangent plane.\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{3}-3x+{y}^{2}+2y) \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}-3 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{3}-3x+{y}^{2}+2y) \\\\  & =2y+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nHence, the normal vector to the tangent plane is \n\n$$\n⟨3{x}^{2}-3,\\,2y+2,\\,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nNote that the tangent plane is horizontal if and only if the normal vector is vertical. This means that the $x$- and $y$-components of the vector must be $0:$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=0 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-3=0 \\\\ 2y+2=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFrom the first equation, we get $x=\\pm 1$ while the second equation gives $y=-1.$ \n\nFinally, substituting $x=-1$ and $y=-1$ into our function, we get\n\n$$\nz=(-1)^{3}-3(-1)+(-1)^{2}+2(-1)=1.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=1$ and $y=-1$ into our function, we get\n\n$$\nz=(1)^{3}-3(1)+(-1)^{2}+2(-1)=-3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the points where the tangent plane is horizontal are $(-1,-1,1)$ and $(1,-1,-3).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403723 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "58427e6a-8e77-5c01-a578-a7dc4b8f8812" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Projection of the Intersection of a Surface and a Tilted Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b46c7ae5-0a46-59a6-bf83-e94f8ccd0940" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,-2)$ in the $xz$-plane" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403725} {:answer/id #uuid "9566cc47-e8d9-5640-b0dc-201689f1ab94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-1,5)$ in the $xz$-plane" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403726} {:answer/id #uuid "2357d2af-705d-5e3c-9928-fdd9ee85001e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,-5)$ in the $xz$-plane" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403727} {:answer/id #uuid "761cef9d-c588-526d-a20b-157cfe62168e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,0)$ in the $xz$-plane" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403728} {:answer/id #uuid "27a115d9-7ebe-5bc3-87bb-45cfdc28a5ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-1,2)$ in the $xz$-plane" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403729}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b46c7ae5-0a46-59a6-bf83-e94f8ccd0940" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,-2)$ in the $xz$-plane" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403725} :answer-field/id #uuid "635cbbf9-c9f6-5df5-ac89-d0b4c298244a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403724}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cc9a60c0-9b27-537d-8f7e-b89ae32845f5" :question/math-academy-id "q-73866" :question/problem "For the elliptic paraboloid $\\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{4}+{y}^{2}=z+5$, the section in the plane $\\sqrt{3}x-2y=2\\sqrt{3}$ is a parabola. \nFind the vertex of the projection of this parabola onto $xz$-plane." :question/worked-solution "Since we want to find the projection onto the $xz$-plane, we need to eliminate the variable $y.$ First, we solve for $y$ in the equation of the plane, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{3}x-2y=2\\sqrt{3}\\,⟹\\,y=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}(x-2)}{2}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the expression for $y$ into the equation of the paraboloid, we obtain: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{4}+{(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}(x-2)}{2})}^{2} & =z+5 \\\\ \\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{3(x-2)^{2}}{4} & =z+5 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}+4x+4}{4}+\\frac{3{x}^{2}-12x+12}{4} & =z+5 \\\\ \\frac{4{x}^{2}-8x+16}{4} & =z+5 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x+4 & =z+5 \\\\ z & ={x}^{2}-2x-1 \\\\ z & =({x}^{2}-2x+1)-2 \\\\ z & ={(x-1)}^{2}-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the vertex of the projection onto $xz$-plane is $(1,-2).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403730 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9cc25edf-9d32-5b47-ae4f-e370d2d0d024" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points at Which Tangent Planes Have Particular Properties"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c645d1ad-7209-5343-8f48-3586d833b4f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-1,0,-1)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403732} {:answer/id #uuid "d2196f73-bb73-51d9-8fdd-442f665c7869" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-1,0,-1)$ and $(1,0,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403733} {:answer/id #uuid "af9a2b62-523f-5ba8-b214-6d70686e54b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(2,0,2)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403734} {:answer/id #uuid "892681cb-994a-563c-a47e-7b8ed91ea9ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-2,0,-2)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403735} {:answer/id #uuid "0dfb3a1a-3a3a-528f-9c59-2f8a8f352f97" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-2,0,-2)$ and $(2,0,2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403736}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "892681cb-994a-563c-a47e-7b8ed91ea9ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-2,0,-2)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403735} :answer-field/id #uuid "72aa1076-cce3-53b6-97d2-101a723b64a3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403731}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4dda47ef-07a9-5b66-b0c0-061f88baf1dd" :question/math-academy-id "q-73869" :question/problem "At which points on the surface $z=x{e}^{y}+{y}^{2}$ is the tangent plane perpendicular to the vector  $v\\,=\\,⟨-2,4,2⟩?$" :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z=f(x,y)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ on the surface can be written as\n\n$$\nz-{z}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\n$$\n\nwhere $⟨\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),-1⟩$ represents a normal vector to the tangent plane.\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{e}^{y}+{y}^{2}) \\\\  & ={e}^{y} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{e}^{y}+{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =x{e}^{y}+2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the normal vector to the tangent plane is \n\n$$\n⟨{e}^{y},\\,x{e}^{y}+2y,\\,-1⟩\n$$\n\nOur tangent plane should be perpendicular to $⟨-2,4,2⟩.$ So, the normal vector of that plane must be parallel to that vector. In other words, for some constant $c$, we must have\n\n$$\nc\\cdot ⟨{e}^{y},\\,x{e}^{y}+2y,\\,-1⟩=⟨-2,4,2⟩.\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding coordinates, we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}c{e}^{y}=-2 \\\\ cx{e}^{y}+2cy=4 \\\\ -c=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get $c=-2.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n-2{e}^{y}=-2\\,⟹\\,y=0.\n$$\n\nAnd substituting $c=-2$ and $y=0$ into the second equation, we have \n\n$$\n-2x{e}^{0}+2(-2)(0)=4\\,⟹\\,x=-2.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x=-2$ and $y=0$ into our function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =x{e}^{y}+{y}^{2} \\\\  & =(-2){e}^{0}+{0}^{2} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent plane is perpendicular to the vector $v$ at $(-2,0,-2).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403737 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f2f6a868-46fe-5529-a464-b6f3e0243d30" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Singular 3x3 System of Equations That Has No Solution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8f607536-ba89-5718-aed4-73d79d8fefe2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+y[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]+z[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,y,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403739} {:answer/id #uuid "c6da98ac-d2bd-5e17-bf72-7dbf0e0b1406" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+y[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]+z[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,y,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403740} {:answer/id #uuid "7102331a-61ac-555b-a308-ed6c3c607afc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403741} {:answer/id #uuid "fe9dc8bc-d50e-5f42-a53c-d906ba0fc3e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+y[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]+z[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,y,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403742} {:answer/id #uuid "e0aa2a8a-523a-5375-aabf-871e94803607" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+y[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]+z[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,y,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403743}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7102331a-61ac-555b-a308-ed6c3c607afc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403741} :answer-field/id #uuid "154dd871-921f-589c-a014-a6f06b889ded" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403738}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "546eeb31-583e-555f-a75b-4f974cd8d508" :question/math-academy-id "q-73953" :question/problem "Consider the augmented matrix $M$ given by \n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 4 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 10\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the general solution of the linear system represented by the matrix." :question/worked-solution "The system corresponding to $M$ is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}3x+5y+2z=1 \\\\ 0x-2y+4z=3 \\\\ 0x+0y+0z=10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince there are no such numbers $x,y,z$ that form a solution of the final equation $0x+0y+0z=10,$ we can conclude that our system has no solution."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403744 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "abef3da6-252e-51e4-b816-9ef7233b9a5d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intersection Point of a Line in Vector Form and a Plane in Dot Product Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7d6e560d-c0f9-5b00-9374-501b77816db3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-5,-6,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403746} {:answer/id #uuid "0d5dad58-6f17-557a-a47c-3daedf8cd066" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,-6,7)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403747} {:answer/id #uuid "697db66d-1d20-570e-bd42-19a3bff15801" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No intersection" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403748} {:answer/id #uuid "2a612b0e-faeb-5123-a818-5a85e0cc7103" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-5,2,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403749} {:answer/id #uuid "f4069451-0a02-5138-bd42-c9a8964d8236" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,2,7)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403750}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "697db66d-1d20-570e-bd42-19a3bff15801" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No intersection" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403748} :answer-field/id #uuid "b8a46fca-92cb-569e-aaa5-ff42267626c0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403745}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "36894dd9-ad4f-58c5-8fd6-dfd9706bcecb" :question/math-academy-id "q-73984" :question/problem "Find the intersection point of the line $l$ and the plane $Π$ with equations\n\n$$\nl:r=⟨3,2,7⟩+t⟨-5,-6,0⟩,\\,Π:r\\cdot ⟨-6,5,4⟩=-24.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's write the equation of the line as\n\n$$\nr=⟨3-5t,2-6t,7⟩.\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $r=⟨3-5t,2-6t,7⟩$ into the equation of the plane, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r\\cdot ⟨-6,5,4⟩ & =-24 \\\\ ⟨3-5t,2-6t,7⟩\\cdot ⟨-6,5,4⟩ & =-24 \\\\ -6(3-5t)+5(2-6t)+4\\cdot 7 & =-24 \\\\ -18+30t+10-30t+28 & =-24 \\\\ 20 & =-24.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation has no solution. Therefore, we conclude that the line $l$ and the plane $Π$ do not intersect."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403751 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "82e761b5-ec1c-549d-b726-e0909cfe460b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intersection Point of a Line in Parametric Form and a Plane in Cartesian Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f8e6222-7ae2-547f-adb4-60dcb2768d17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(10,12,11)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403753} {:answer/id #uuid "d2075349-1cd9-5618-bf54-c666bd8b0fb5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,0,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403754} {:answer/id #uuid "4e82ca3b-87f3-5e2d-81bb-50cb54054a36" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No intersection" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403755} {:answer/id #uuid "99dfd052-8843-53bb-aa22-298fbe240e23" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,3,11)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403756} {:answer/id #uuid "f0a8b821-3ef2-5ef9-b738-52d815031a67" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,15,11)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403757}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f0a8b821-3ef2-5ef9-b738-52d815031a67" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,15,11)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403757} :answer-field/id #uuid "0b483925-643c-505a-9163-d50ee76306cd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403752}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e45ecd53-082b-51d5-8bee-c71442a1f3b8" :question/math-academy-id "q-73999" :question/problem "Find the intersection point of the line $l$ and the plane $Π$ with equations\n\n$$\nl:{\\begin{aligned}x=-2-t \\\\ y=9-3t \\\\ z=-1-6t\\end{aligned}\\,\\text{and}\\,Π:-3x+2y+5z-85=0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We can substitute the parametric expressions for $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ of the line into the equation of the plane immediately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3x+2y+5z-85 & =0 \\\\ -3(-2-t)+2(9-3t)+5(-1-6t) & =85 \\\\ 6+3t+18-6t-5-30t & =85 \\\\ -33t & =66 \\\\ t & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo get the position vector $p$ of the intersection point $P$, we substitute $t=-2$ into the parametric equations of the line:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=-2-(-2)=0 \\\\ y=9-3(-2)=15 \\\\ z=-1-6(-2)=11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection point is  $P(0,15,11).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403758 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ce8958e6-c578-572f-8b34-5513b6f64a51" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Orthogonal Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c7bba809-4721-5c7c-a48f-bfd2433737e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$w$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403760} {:answer/id #uuid "43d9e4d1-8fd6-51da-a82d-2abfd7b01414" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$v$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403761} {:answer/id #uuid "0d4b2021-aa4e-51fe-8f3b-7c38e32a9c2f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$v$ and $w$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403762} {:answer/id #uuid "52589bc7-27ee-59f4-9207-199b2fca508f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403763} {:answer/id #uuid "d2dc313f-9b1a-5d2b-9aaf-9e6ff1625af1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$v$ and $x$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403764}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0d4b2021-aa4e-51fe-8f3b-7c38e32a9c2f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$v$ and $w$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403762} :answer-field/id #uuid "a31d4961-a16e-58ba-afd9-33d1a3426a24" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403759}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "96a85d0f-f840-5482-ab7d-823e5b207944" :question/math-academy-id "q-74089" :question/problem "Given the vector $u=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]$, which of the following vectors are orthogonal to $u?$\n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,w=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We compute the dot product between $u$ and each of the listed vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot v & ={u}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}+{u}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{2}+{u}_{3}\\cdot {v}_{3}+{u}_{4}\\cdot {v}_{4} \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-2)+(-1)\\cdot 1+3\\cdot 0+5\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-4-1+0+5 \\\\  & =0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot w & ={u}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{1}+{u}_{2}\\cdot {w}_{2}+{u}_{3}\\cdot {w}_{3}+{u}_{4}\\cdot {w}_{4} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1+(-1)\\cdot 4+3\\cdot (-1)+5\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2-4-3+5 \\\\  & =0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot x & ={u}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{1}+{u}_{2}\\cdot {x}_{2}+{u}_{3}\\cdot {x}_{3}+{u}_{4}\\cdot {x}_{4} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 2+(-1)\\cdot 0+3\\cdot (-2)+5\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =4+0-6-5 \\\\  & =-7.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the correct option is $v$ and $w$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403765 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ae41d849-9def-540c-8985-8fbeec679991" :knowledge-point/title "Normalizing a Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5eb8254f-2c6f-524e-99dd-34023e51e73a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=\\pm 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403767} {:answer/id #uuid "019fec80-5acc-501a-bef4-14b614fa816f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=\\pm 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403768} {:answer/id #uuid "8fe0cf0a-bc52-5146-808d-133a9bc497f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=1,\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403769} {:answer/id #uuid "53f8d4f7-7db3-5626-bdac-49666459596b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=\\pm \\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403770} {:answer/id #uuid "7b1abf38-83af-5bd0-bde3-158e2b0e01d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=1,3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403771}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8fe0cf0a-bc52-5146-808d-133a9bc497f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=1,\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403769} :answer-field/id #uuid "e9b0cd10-86a5-5799-a64d-9f35511d1e34" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403766}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "de94dd8c-d1ac-5e3c-a7d7-a9749972614e" :question/math-academy-id "q-74151" :question/problem "Let $x=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -k \\\\ 2k \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ and $y=[\\begin{aligned}k \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].$ Find $k$, if the distance between the vectors is $\\text{d}(x,y)=2\\sqrt{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the distance between $x$ and $y$ in terms of $k:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}(x,y) & =‖x-y‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{({x}_{1}-{y}_{1})^{2}+({x}_{2}-{y}_{2})^{2}+({x}_{3}-{y}_{3})^{2}+({x}_{4}-{y}_{4})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(3-k)^{2}+(-k-(-1))^{2}+(2k-0)^{2}+(0-(-2))^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9-6k+{k}^{2}+{k}^{2}-2k+1+4{k}^{2}+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6{k}^{2}-8k+14}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the given equation  $\\text{d}(x,y)=2\\sqrt{3},$ we obtain an equation that we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{6{k}^{2}-8k+14} & =2\\sqrt{3} \\\\ {(\\sqrt{6{k}^{2}-8k+14})}^{2} & ={(2\\sqrt{3})}^{2} \\\\ 6{k}^{2}-8k+14 & =12 \\\\ 6{k}^{2}-8k+2 & =0 \\\\ 3{k}^{2}-4k+1 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the quadratic formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k & =\\frac{-b\\pm \\sqrt{{b}^{2}-4ac}}{2a} \\\\  & =\\frac{-(-4)\\pm \\sqrt{(-4)^{2}-4(3)(1)}}{2(3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\pm \\sqrt{4}}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\pm 2}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pm 1}{3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives $k=1$ and $k=\\frac{1}{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403772 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a04b551f-3323-5b91-b01e-941912cc92be" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Vector Lies on a Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e98f85e7-255d-57c4-987f-635dd16edd05" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403774} {:answer/id #uuid "22eae911-bfae-55d1-acf2-f7cf5e10e29a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403775} {:answer/id #uuid "a100b320-8f04-5bdd-8ea7-26f1cec017c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403776} {:answer/id #uuid "b4645d11-c80f-53cc-afba-0d87d9de8fa5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403777} {:answer/id #uuid "d43a8623-8d3d-5185-9200-86c7e4e420c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$u$ does not lie on $Π$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403778}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d43a8623-8d3d-5185-9200-86c7e4e420c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$u$ does not lie on $Π$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403778} :answer-field/id #uuid "b97ac01d-f426-551c-8b2f-1c4b7bdf001e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403773}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "da2b75e4-b38a-5b5b-9891-19906308bd00" :question/math-academy-id "q-74600" :question/problem "Determine if the vector $u$ lies on the plane $Π:r=sb+tc$, $t,s\\in R$, where  $b=⟨-1,4,-5⟩$, $c=⟨3,-9,15⟩$, and  $u=⟨2,-2,11⟩.$ In the affirmative case, find the value of $s\\cdot t$ from the decomposition $u=sb+tc.$" :question/worked-solution "By definition, $u\\in Π$ if there exist $t$ and $s$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sb+tc & =u \\\\ s[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -9 \\\\ 15\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 11\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-s+3t=2 \\\\ 4s-9t=-2 \\\\ -5s+15t=11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve the system above, we set up and row reduce an augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 4 & -9 & -2 \\\\ -5 & 15 & 11\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 6 \\\\ -5 & 15 & 11\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-5{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe third row of the augmented matrix now corresponds to the equation $0{x}_{1}+0{x}_{2}=1,$ which has no solution.\n\nSo, there are no values of $s$ and $t$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sb+tc=u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $u$ does *not* lie on the plane $Π.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403779 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b010a518-7aa3-5b53-9a6d-ef0779ff0dfa" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Reduced Row Echelon Form of a Singular 3x3 System With Two Pivots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c75e1a37-431f-56e2-b6cd-917ed934bc54" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}10z-1 \\\\ 1-4z \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403781} {:answer/id #uuid "913376ed-a5cb-5479-ab16-e42eb401612f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5z-1 \\\\ 1-4z \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403782} {:answer/id #uuid "082f2000-223e-5c35-8880-3c877b9f58a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}10z+1 \\\\ 1+4z \\\\ z+1\\end{aligned}],\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403783} {:answer/id #uuid "c23159c4-b1db-556c-a013-ffc4b8987ced" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}10z-1 \\\\ 1-4z \\\\ z+1\\end{aligned}],\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403784} {:answer/id #uuid "8545a78e-5e9f-5188-9a89-b26fb6b7b34d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5z-1 \\\\ 1-2z \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403785}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c75e1a37-431f-56e2-b6cd-917ed934bc54" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}10z-1 \\\\ 1-4z \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403781} :answer-field/id #uuid "7014ab46-6268-5d03-a678-37850fc6da0a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403780}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "23bca3c5-322d-5c3f-a423-fb871cdd59a3" :question/math-academy-id "q-74626" :question/problem "Consider the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x+2y-2z=1 \\\\ -3x-6y+6z=-3 \\\\ 2x+5y=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the standard Gauss-Jordan method to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to reduced row echelon form, and then solve the linear system." :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -2 & 1 \\\\ -3 & -6 & 6 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTransforming $M$ into reduced row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -2 & 1 \\\\ -3 & -6 & 6 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & ↔{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the row echelon form above, we have only two pivots (in the $1$st and $2$nd columns). To transform the matrix to reduced row echelon form, we need to create zeros above the pivots only. This gives,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -10 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x-10z=-1 \\\\ y+4z=1 \\\\ 0=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince any numbers $x,y,z$ form a solution of the final equation, we can remove this equation from the system. \n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variables are $x$ and $y,$ and we have one free variable, $z.$ That means we need to find $x$ and $y$ in terms of $z.$ \n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=10z-1 \\\\ y=1-4z \\\\ z=z,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $z$ (the free variable) is an arbitrary number.\n\nIn vector form, the solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}10z-1 \\\\ 1-4z \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403786 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b010a518-7aa3-5b53-9a6d-ef0779ff0dfa" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Reduced Row Echelon Form of a Singular 3x3 System With Two Pivots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "475078d1-c1fe-5714-8a98-996d56413264" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2z-3 \\\\ 3z-4 \\\\ z+4\\end{aligned}],\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403788} {:answer/id #uuid "2e6ffd15-8092-5ab7-b409-b0f534768790" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2z-3 \\\\ 3z-4 \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403789} {:answer/id #uuid "8ac9d520-f7c9-530e-8570-80e1a9c80366" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2z+3 \\\\ 3z-4 \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403790} {:answer/id #uuid "5ef65d53-9191-5c7b-8a89-8ef906f99da4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2z+3 \\\\ 3z+4 \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403791} {:answer/id #uuid "9129b373-d01f-517b-921a-56a705f741ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2z+3 \\\\ 3z+4 \\\\ z-2\\end{aligned}],\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403792}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5ef65d53-9191-5c7b-8a89-8ef906f99da4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2z+3 \\\\ 3z+4 \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403791} :answer-field/id #uuid "181c2fee-3197-54a6-a09d-7ecb85a2a180" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403787}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "75dbc97d-95b7-5046-a4b7-609e3df9b396" :question/math-academy-id "q-74628" :question/problem "Consider the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x-2z=3 \\\\ 7x-14z=21 \\\\ 2y-6z=8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the standard Gauss-Jordan method to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to reduced row echelon form, and then solve the linear system." :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 7 & 0 & -14 & 21 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -6 & 8\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTransforming $M$ into reduced row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 7 & 0 & -14 & 21 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -6 & 8\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-7){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -6 & 8\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & ↔{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -6 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x-2z=3 \\\\ y-3z=4 \\\\ 0=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince any numbers $x,y,z$ form a solution of the final equation, we can remove this equation from the system. \n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variables are $x$ and $y,$ and we have one free variable, $z.$ That means we need to find $x$ and $y$ in terms of $z.$ \n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=2z+3 \\\\ y=3z+4 \\\\ z=z,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $z$ (the free variable) is an arbitrary number.\n\nIn vector form, the solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2z+3 \\\\ 3z+4 \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403793 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f9469d5c-e097-590c-8ddb-2c7fda353fac" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying a System as Consistent Dependent, Consistent Independent, or Inconsistent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b9541786-a906-58e9-a152-185ff736eaa6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403795} {:answer/id #uuid "4fb03056-50c5-5118-adbd-9eed619e27a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403796} {:answer/id #uuid "735a983f-bd94-50e7-8c94-79524d3dc890" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403797} {:answer/id #uuid "1f61f036-cb15-5c0c-9077-194388e6d6e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403798} {:answer/id #uuid "d3a62366-a32a-5255-9a90-55ea9e42bab4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403799}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "735a983f-bd94-50e7-8c94-79524d3dc890" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403797} :answer-field/id #uuid "f91e44eb-099d-52c4-a904-f2a31b9b0fa8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403794}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a8971116-e24d-5808-9b29-a50e168511e5" :question/math-academy-id "q-74634" :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations.\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & 2x-y=-1 \\\\  & 2x-4y=3 \\\\  & x+y=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The system is consistent dependent\nII. The system has a unique solution\nIII. The system is inconsistent" :question/worked-solution "To classify the system, we will first try to find its solution. The augmented matrix for this system is \n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -4 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nUsing the method of Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -4 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{3}{2} & \\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\frac{9}{2}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe final row corresponds to the equation $0x+0y=\\frac{9}{2},$ and it has no solution. Therefore, our system has no solution, and we conclude that the system is inconsistent.\n\nAs a result, only statement III is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403800 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3f0e2596-73d6-540c-af84-b19a943da6a8" :knowledge-point/title "The Second Derivative of a Vector-Valued Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ca92dd1-d3b1-5bbd-87a9-9caff1cfe1bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{{t}^{3}}},\\,\\frac{-3}{(t+1)^{2}}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403802} {:answer/id #uuid "eaf9ebfc-20a2-5603-a31d-0792c9a03622" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{-3}{t+1},\\,-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403803} {:answer/id #uuid "7ecb4957-d24d-59f5-9347-136d1afee955" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{3}{(t+1)^{2}},\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{t}^{3}}}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403804} {:answer/id #uuid "b63167ac-944e-5401-84cf-f23779d4b9e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{-3}{(t+1)^{2}},\\,-\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{{t}^{3}}}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403805} {:answer/id #uuid "ccf003b1-60be-57fa-890c-82515b81d733" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{-1}{t+1},\\,-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{t}^{3}}}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403806}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b63167ac-944e-5401-84cf-f23779d4b9e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{-3}{(t+1)^{2}},\\,-\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{{t}^{3}}}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403805} :answer-field/id #uuid "3ab71387-0269-5f3f-bf93-cd7cf7c2275e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403801}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "40db78a3-8e98-5f3c-8f6f-0721104f563f" :question/math-academy-id "q-75034" :question/problem "Given that $r(t)=⟨3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+1),\\,\\sqrt{t}⟩,$ ${r}^{″}(t)=$" :question/worked-solution "To find the second derivative of a vector-valued function, we find the second derivatives of the components.\n\n- For $x(t)=3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+1),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{3}{t+1} \\\\ {x}^{″}(t) & =-\\frac{3}{(t+1)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For $y(t)=\\sqrt{t},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}} \\\\ {y}^{″}(t) & =-\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{{t}^{3}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨{x}^{″}(t),\\,{y}^{″}(t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{3}{(t+1)^{2}},\\,-\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{{t}^{3}}}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403807 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c91fb2b-5607-555d-b897-f0e76765dfa6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Lower Triangular Matrix Given the Elementary Row Operations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "523c2229-b400-5c7a-8577-639c6b8af789" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403809} {:answer/id #uuid "63eae5d2-78e3-5f74-b5e4-a5410101683d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403810} {:answer/id #uuid "40fafdc2-d2ba-5785-a820-7bc80cae9803" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403811} {:answer/id #uuid "53c00d30-d1f5-5321-b731-b01cf5c82916" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403812} {:answer/id #uuid "69e79a65-9bd9-5bb6-9162-647d1a1434fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403813}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "69e79a65-9bd9-5bb6-9162-647d1a1434fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403813} :answer-field/id #uuid "b9f95a1a-2498-5647-bcee-22f3ad54c88b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403808}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c7bd6831-c61b-5654-9e45-1822cb85cf38" :question/math-academy-id "q-75099" :question/problem "Suppose that a $3\\times 3$ matrix $H$ is reduced to row echelon form using the following elementary row operations, applied in the order in which they appear:\n\n$$\n{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1},\\,{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $H$ has the LU factorization $H=LU,$ then $L=$" :question/worked-solution "Applying ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2)\\cdot {R}_{1}$ and then ${R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+3\\cdot {R}_{1}$ is equivalent to multiplying $H$ from the left by the following elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{E}_{2}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{E}_{1}}\\cdot H=U\n$$\n\nNow, since ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}H=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{2}$ and ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}H & =U \\\\ {E}_{1}H & ={E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ H & ={E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ H & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ H & =([\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}])U \\\\ H & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}\\cdot U\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403814 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c91fb2b-5607-555d-b897-f0e76765dfa6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Lower Triangular Matrix Given the Elementary Row Operations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "53040db9-250a-56a5-aaac-6f9de1208aeb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 4 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403816} {:answer/id #uuid "38d13893-1722-5c2a-ae81-602f74f9eec5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403817} {:answer/id #uuid "61f534ac-fe4c-590c-9a9b-8f99140fd7f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403818} {:answer/id #uuid "94317c8c-0c37-5d0c-8721-a8b615d86c75" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403819} {:answer/id #uuid "be9b0408-7cf7-5c46-b6d4-9c65348a7946" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -4 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403820}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "61f534ac-fe4c-590c-9a9b-8f99140fd7f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403818} :answer-field/id #uuid "f08b2c1b-6e45-549d-8e75-f22a5e13ebd5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403815}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3ec22232-a046-59e8-a23e-1122c9545c1c" :question/math-academy-id "q-75100" :question/problem "Suppose that a $3\\times 3$ matrix $H$ is reduced to row echelon form using the following elementary row operations, applied in the order in which they appear:\n\n$$\n{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+4{R}_{1},\\,{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-4{R}_{1}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $H$ has the LU factorization $H=LU,$ then $L=$" :question/worked-solution "Applying ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+4\\cdot {R}_{1}$ and then ${R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-4)\\cdot {R}_{1}$ is equivalent to multiplying $H$ from the left by the following elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{E}_{2}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{E}_{1}}\\cdot H=U\n$$\n\nNow, since ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}H=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{2}$ and ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}H & =U \\\\ {E}_{1}H & ={E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ H & ={E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ H & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ H & =([\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}])U \\\\ H & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}\\cdot U\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
